# RFC-0006: Admin SPA

Status: Active

## Abstract

The Raytha admin is a React single-page app served at `/raytha` from
`src/Raytha.Web/wwwroot/raytha`. It authenticates with the same cookie as the
rest of the host and talks only to `/raytha/api/auth` and `/raytha/api/admin`.
New admin work goes into the SPA, not into the Razor admin pages.

## 1. Workspace

`src/admin` is a pnpm workspace (pnpm 11, Node 24):

| Package | Role |
|---|---|
| `apps/shell` (`@raytha/shell`) | The admin app: router, pages, layout |
| `packages/api` (`@raytha/api`) | Typed fetch client, session cache, response/error types |
| `packages/ui` (`@raytha/ui`) | Shared components, the only place with Vitest tests today |

- HTTP calls MUST go through `@raytha/api`. Raw `fetch` in a page or component
  is FORBIDDEN — that client owns base paths, JSON headers, error mapping, and
  the session cache the route guards read.
- Shared visual components belong in `@raytha/ui`. A component copied into
  `apps/shell` because it was faster is a defect.
- TypeScript is strict; `any` and non-null assertions need a stated reason.
  `pnpm typecheck` and `pnpm lint` MUST pass (RFC-0007).

## 2. Routing

- TanStack Router with `basepath: "/raytha"` and `trailingSlash: "never"`. URLs
  look like `/raytha/users`, `/raytha/content-types/new`; the dashboard is
  `/raytha` with no trailing slash.
- Server-owned paths under `/raytha` are listed once in
  `AdminSpaExtensions.ServerPathPrefixes` and mirrored in the Vite dev proxy
  (`apps/shell/vite.config.ts`). Adding a server path means editing **both**, or
  the route works in one environment and 404s in the other.
- The SPA fallback is `MapGet("/raytha/{**path}")`, deliberately not
  `MapFallback`, so it sits below literal-segment endpoints and above the public
  site's `{*route}` catch-all. Do not convert it to `MapFallback`.
- Data loading is TanStack Query. Query keys MUST include every input that
  changes the result (ids, page, search, sort), and mutations MUST invalidate the
  lists they affect rather than mutating cache by hand.

## 3. Admin API

- Endpoints live in `src/Raytha.Web/Areas/Admin/Api`, one static class per
  resource, wired from `AdminApiEndpoints.MapAdminApi`.
- The `/raytha/api/admin` group requires `RaythaClaimTypes.IsAdmin`; each
  resource group narrows further with the same `BuiltInSystemPermission` or
  content-type policy the Razor admin used (RFC-0008). An endpoint with no
  `RequireAuthorization` inherits the group's — never add `AllowAnonymous` to an
  admin resource.
- Endpoints MUST be thin: bind, send through `ISender`, shape with
  `AdminResults`. Business logic belongs in a handler (RFC-0002).
- List endpoints bind `[AsParameters] PagedQuery`
  (`?pageNumber=&pageSize=&orderBy=&search=`) and return via
  `AdminResults.Paged` (RFC-0010).
- Verbs: `GET` collection and item, `POST` create, `PUT` full update, `DELETE`
  remove, `POST /{id}/{action}` for state changes (`suspend`, `restore`,
  `reset-password`). Ids in paths are `ShortGuid` strings.
- Errors are RFC 7807 problem details — every `/raytha/api/*` path answers with
  `application/problem+json`, produced by `AdminResults` or
  `ExceptionsMiddleware`. Do not invent a bespoke error envelope.
- `/raytha/api/auth` owns sign-in, sign-out, `me`, schemes, magic link, forgot
  password, and first-run setup. Only `me` requires an admin claim.

## 4. Auth and Mutations

- Authentication is the host's cookie session; sign-in through
  `POST /raytha/api/auth/login` issues the same principal as the Razor admin
  login, and `CustomCookieAuthenticationEvents` hydrates the full claim set on
  the next request. The SPA MUST NOT store tokens in `localStorage`.
- Under `/raytha/api`, an unauthenticated request gets a status code, not an HTML
  redirect. Route guards use the cached session from `@raytha/api`
  (`isAuthenticated`), backed by `GET /raytha/api/auth/me`.
- Mutating requests (`POST`, `PUT`, `PATCH`) to `/raytha/api/admin` and
  `/raytha/api/auth` MUST send `Content-Type: application/json`.
  `JsonRequestGuardMiddleware` answers 415 otherwise; that guard is what replaces
  antiforgery tokens for these endpoints, so form-encoded admin mutations are
  FORBIDDEN.
- Permission-gated UI reads `permissions` from `me` via `hasPermission`. Hiding a
  control is presentation only — the server-side policy is the control.

## 5. Build and Commit

- Development: `./tools/dev.sh` runs the host on `http://localhost:5200` and
  auto-starts Vite on `5203`; the host proxies SPA paths to Vite so HMR works.
  `AdminSpa__AutoStart=false` serves the committed bundle instead.
- Production: Vite builds to `src/Raytha.Web/wwwroot/raytha`
  (`base: "/raytha/"`, so assets resolve under `/raytha/assets/…`).
- **The built bundle is committed.** After changing anything under `src/admin`,
  run `pnpm build` in `src/admin` and stage `src/Raytha.Web/wwwroot/raytha` in
  the same commit. CI fails on a stale bundle
  (`git diff --exit-code -- src/Raytha.Web/wwwroot/raytha`).
- Do not hand-edit files in `wwwroot/raytha`.

## 6. Razor Admin

The Razor admin pages under `Areas/Admin/Pages` still exist for parity during
the 2.0 transition, and which one answers a given `/raytha` path is decided by
route precedence, not by configuration:

- In Development with Vite running, the dev proxy runs before routing and is
  authoritative for every SPA path, so you see the React app.
- Serving the committed bundle instead (`AdminSpa__AutoStart=false`, or any
  non-Development environment), Razor wins wherever it has a route. That includes
  `/raytha` itself, every literal Razor page, and — because `ContentItems` maps
  `/raytha/{contentTypeDeveloperName}/{viewId?}` — every remaining one- and
  two-segment path, since a parameter segment outranks the SPA's `{**path}`
  catch-all. Measured against a signed-in admin on the bundle, `/raytha` returns
  the Razor dashboard and `/raytha/webhooks` redirects to `/raytha/error/404`;
  no current SPA route renders.

Consequences to plan around: the SPA is a Development-through-Vite experience
today, and a page that works under `pnpm dev` is shadowed in a built deployment.
Retiring a Razor page (or narrowing that content-items route) is what hands a URL
to the SPA. The `admin_spa` flag in the catalog (RFC-0009) is a placeholder for
that cutover and gates nothing today — do not write code that assumes it does.

Fixes MAY be applied to the Razor admin; new features MUST be built in the SPA.
Do not add a Razor admin page for something the SPA should own, and remember that
adding one silently takes a URL away from the SPA.
